---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AprCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/AprCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="apr-calculator">
  <AprCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the APR calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The payment comes from the loan amount, the interest rate and the term:
      <code>M = P × i ÷ (1 − (1 + i)^−n)</code>. Fees reduce the money you receive (or increase the loan when they are
      added to it). The APR calculator finds the monthly rate <code>j</code> at which the payments are worth exactly
      the money you receive, <code>received = M × (1 − (1 + j)^−n) ÷ j</code>, and reports <code>APR = 12 × j</code>.
      It solves for <code>j</code> by bisection.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 25,000 loan at 7.5% for 5 years has a payment of 500.95 and 5,056.96 of interest. If 1,000 of fees come out of
      the loan, you receive 24,000 and the APR is 9.236%. If the fees are added to the loan instead, the payment is
      520.99 and the APR is 9.167%.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The result is for a fixed-rate loan with level monthly payments, kept to the end. Which fees count toward a
      legal APR depends on the country and the loan type. If you repay early, the fees are spread over fewer years and
      the real yearly cost is higher.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
